<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udmurts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Votyaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Udmurts are a Finno-Ugric people of the lands between the Vyatka and Kama rivers, most of whom live in the Udmurt Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with its capital at Izhevsk. They came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>, and most were absorbed into Russia after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552. Their Permic language is closely related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udmurts.jpg" alt="Udmurts" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sirelius, U. T. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Udmurts_1907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Udmurts are a Permian, Finno-Ugric people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> who speak the Udmurt language, a member of the Uralic family. Their name derives from a Permic form meaning meadow people, built from the root for meadow, glade or turf and murt, person, probably an early borrowing from an Iranian language. A document of 1557 calls them lugovye lyudi, 'meadow people', beside the traditional Russian name votyaki. Another theory derives the name wholly from Proto-Iranian, meaning resident of the outskirts or border zone.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> they were mainly called Chud Otyatskaya, Otyaks, Wotyaks or Votyaks. Udmurts today consider these names offensive. They are closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komis</a> to their north, both in language and culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Udmurts live in Udmurtia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where they make up 24.1% of the population, with small groups in Kirov Oblast, Perm Krai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a> and Mari El. The Russian census counted 746,562 in 1989 and 552,299 in 2010.</p><p>The Udmurts used to be semi-nomadic forest dwellers living in riverside communities, but most now live in towns. Their clan-based social structure no longer exists, though its traces remain strong and continue to shape Udmurt culture. They have a national epic, Dorvyzhy, and national instruments include the krez, a zither similar to the Russian gusli, and a pipe-like wind instrument, the chipchirghan. A chapter of the 1776 book Description de toutes les nations de l'empire de Russie describes the Wotyak people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-frazer">James George Frazer</a> mentions a rite of theirs in The Golden Bough. About 70% of Udmurt men carry haplogroup N, a pattern common among Uralic-speaking peoples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Udmurts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…centuries these groups settled in the forest-steppe around the confluence of the Volga and the Kama, in what is now Tatarstan, and established their rule over the Finnic and other peoples of the region, among them ancestors of the Mari, <b>Udmurts</b> and Mordvins, as well as the Burtas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kazan ruled Tatars, Chuvash, Mari, <b>Udmurts</b> and Bashkirs, with a farming and trading economy linked to the Volga route.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers from Novgorod and other Russian lands moved into the Vyatka basin among the <b>Udmurt</b> and Mari peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/vyatka-land">Vyatka Land</a></li></ul></section>
